A1. One of the great benefits derived from crossword puzzles is learning that these are elongated fish. ~ EELS
A4. Any married man. ~ SLAVE
A8. Something you don't have to have a hunting license to shoot. ~ CRAP
A11. Initials of an old order of hod-carriers and shillalah experts. ~ AOH
A13. A friend in France. ~ AMI
A14. Something it takes a good sock to hold. ~ TOE
A15. The seemingly unattainable goal of all barber shop quartets. ~ HARMONY
A18. Girls who will soon be attempting to outstrip their predecessors at a coming-out party. ~ SUBDEBS
A21. To marry this number of times is to marry once too often. ~ ONCE
A22. A leader in the movies. ~ USHER
A24. Something grass widows occasionally turn green with. ~ ENVY
A25. A Quaker's pronoun. ~ YE
A26. Where soaks go temporarily when the water wagon starts. ~ ON
A27. A feminine affirmative. ~ NO
A29. A good fiction writer can always get a job as this kind of a writer (abbr.). ~ AD
A31. Barney Google's hobby. ~ NAG
A33. A tail light abbreviation for "slow." ~ SLO
A35. Interjection of indignant inebriates. ~ HIC
A38. A semi-pellucid gem. (Whatever that means!) ~ ONYX
A40. An oft-repeated bedtime story. ~ ALIBI
A42. Something movie directors hitch their wagons to that often turn out to be the shooting variety. ~ STAR
A44. Me and the girl friend. ~ WE
A45. What it is when Witless Willie argues with Dumb Dora. ~ IDIOTICAL
A46. If you could figure out 27 horizontal this will be easy. ~ NO
A47. The only way they could get this fellow to stop his violin practicing was to set the whole town on fire. ~ NERO
A49. You can think of a lot of good crosswords when the dentist starts digging for these. ~ ROOTS
A50. Long, long times. ~ EONS
A52. This is what a ball player becomes when he grows old and his eyesight gets bad. ~ UMP
A54. Persian Seamless Suspenders (abbr.). ~ PSS
A55. An English dumb-bell. ~ ASS
A56. Initials of an American city famous for its earthquakes. ~ SF
A57. Preposition of place. ~ AT
A59. Initials of the order of the knights of the trowel. ~ FM
A60. Tired business men without any business (abbr.). ~ TM
A62. Something a red-headed girl takes when she lights a cork tipped cigarette in front of a barber shop on a windy day. ~ PUFF
A64. Pertaining to the nose, beezer, snoot, horn, proboscis, or what have you? ~ NASAL
A67. An enclosed space. ~ AREA
A69. What a red hot papa does when the elopers return (perhaps). ~ RELENTS
A71. Wooden kimonas. ~ COFFINS
A73. A Sprite. ~ ELF
A74. Something you should do to the line. ~ HUE
A76. The kangaroo's perambulator. ~ SAC
A77. This is usually kept dark if it's shady. ~ PAST
A78. This kind of a fellow has very taking ways, especially when it comes to horses. ~ GYPSY
A79. A South American ostrich. ~ RHEA

D1. The only thing that can have the last word in an argument with one of the fair sex. ~ ECHO
D2. Petting and stealing a kiss. ~ LARCENY
D3. No matter how much money people amass they always want this much more. ~ SOME
D5. Something a hen does. (Careful now!) ~ LAYS
D6. Nowadays this is what girls come home from dances in (abbr.). ~ AM
D7. This has a pair of iron jaws. ~ VISE
D8. Something clandestine lovers write letters in. ~ CODE
D9. To re-establish by law; to institute again. (There's a mean one!) ~ REENACT
D10. This makes a nosegay. (No, Dora, "Gin" is not the word.) ~ POSY
D12. Webster says this is a call to excite attention. ~ HO
D14. Something people go to Denver for. ~ TB
D16. What married man in history wished he didn't have so many wives? ~ ANY
D17. An inmate of a convent. ~ NUN
D19. An ash receiver. ~ URN
D20. What some men like best next to themselves. ~ BVD
D23. If you get this, don't breathe it to a soul. ~ HALITOSIS
D26. The King of Bashan. ~ OG
D28. An exclamation. ~ OH
D30. These are for ladies only, and the reasons for wearing them are becoming quite transparent. ~ GOWNS
D32. What the line about "rolling stones," is. (Applesauce is not the word.) ~ AXIOM
D33. A vessel with one mast. ~ SLOOP
D34. Relating to deaths. (Ask Mr. Webster.) ~ OBITS
D36. Small islands off the west coast of Africa and the east coast of Kohahu. ~ ISLES
D37. A nationally known winter artist. (Just to help you his first name is Jack.) ~ FROST
D39. Born. ~ NEE
D40. This blows about most everything on earth. ~ AIR
D41. Initials of a school in which the pupils hold commencement exercises in the post office. ~ ICS
D43. A girl's name. (Not Yvette.) ~ ANN
D48. Things grandmother wore before she bobbed her hair and took to cigarettes. ~ RUFFLES
D51. A bashful bird. ~ OSTRICH
D53. The old block itself. ~ PA
D55. A milkman's busy period (abbr.). ~ AM
D56. Something landlords do when they raise the rent and their tenants can't raise it. ~ SUE
D58. People go all to pieces over this. ~ TNT
D59. Nickname for Florence. ~ FLO
D61. Products of the great open spaces. ~ MEN
D62. A support. ~ PROP
D63. What the loss of a straw is in September. ~ FELT
D65. Pale. ~ ASHY
D66. These fellows are never safe till they're down and out. ~ ACES
D67. From a distance. ~ AFAR
D68. Land famous for nuts, washers, queues, bandits and Siamese Twins. ~ ASIA
D70. A Canadian Province (abbr.). ~ NF
D72. Flying Sardines (abbr.). ~ FS
D75. What aviators want to keep. ~ UP


